  "overview": {
    "text": "Red is a family of hues perceived when light stimulates vision in a way associated with the long-wavelength edge of the visible spectrum; NASA places that edge near 700 nanometers, beyond which lies invisible infrared. It is not one single wavelength or one fixed pigment: hue varies continuously, and red paints and dyes can be made from different materials. Red’s role as a “primary” depends on the mixing system: it is primary for combining emitted light (RGB), but printing inks use cyan, magenta, and yellow. Its meanings also depend on culture; for example, Smithsonian’s National Museum of Asian Art describes red in China as linked with celebration, prosperity, power, and good fortune.",
    "source_urls": [
      "https://science.nasa.gov/ems/09_visiblelight/",
      "https://aty.sdsu.edu/explain/optics/color/intro.html",
      "https://asia-archive.si.edu/red/"
    ]
  },
  "key_facts": [
    {
      "text": "NASA gives the typical visible-light range as about 380–700 nanometers; red is near its long-wavelength edge, and infrared is beyond what people can see.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://science.nasa.gov/ems/09_visiblelight/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "On screens and in other additive-light systems, red is one of the red-green-blue primaries; red plus green light appears yellow.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://aty.sdsu.edu/explain/optics/color/intro.html"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In subtractive color mixing used for printing, cyan, magenta, and yellow are the primaries; combining magenta and yellow can produce red.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://aty.sdsu.edu/explain/optics/color/intro.html"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Red is not one fixed material: the museum describes vermilion as a red pigment made from cinnabar or produced synthetically, and notes that artworks use varied red pigments and dyes.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://asia-archive.si.edu/red/"
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "In the Chinese contexts described by the National Museum of Asian Art, red has been associated with celebration, prosperity, power, and good fortune; these are cultural meanings, not universal definitions.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://asia-archive.si.edu/red/"
      ]
    }
  ],
